{"context":"EKA has evolved to a CKO/Runtime-centric architecture: the **Runtime API** (ADR-014) is the primary internal boundary — every consumer communicates with the canonical store through the Kernel services (Workspace, Knowledge, Resolver, Relations, Timeline, Snapshot, Integrity) plus the Authoring API, and **Markdown is authoring-only** (one adapter behind the Authoring API, never a runtime representation; ADR-012). Human reading is served by projections (`eka view` / `eka watch`, ADR-013); machine retrieval by `eka get` (ADR-015) — canonical CKO JSON, one Document per object, additive retrieval options over the same Runtime API.\n\nWhat remains unserved is the consumer class the Runtime's existence was shaped for — AI agents, MCP, Atrium. Those consumers do not primarily want one object at a time; they want the answer to a knowledge-shaped question: **\"what Engineering Knowledge do I need to understand this subject correctly?\"** — the higher-authority constraints that bind the subject (decisions, specifications, standards), the one-hop neighborhood it depends on and is referenced by, the decisions and planning around it, its review trail, its instance-line history. ADR-014 §Future Extensibility reserved exactly this slot: *\"Context Engine — `Knowledge.Search` + `Relations` traversal are its query surface; it builds on the Kernel without learning storage\"*; ADR-015 §Future Extensibility repeated it: *\"Context generation — composed Documents for AI-agent context windows, assembled from the same Runtime API surfaces.\"*\n\nThis milestone implements it: **`eka context`** — the **Context Engine** (package `contexts/`), a deterministic Runtime consumer that constructs the **Engineering Context Object** (schema `eka-context-v1`) around ONE focus subject, in three depths (`local` | `dependency` | `engineering`), preserving Knowledge Stratification (ADR-008: strata 1 highest → 5 lowest), classifying the one-hop neighborhood into sections, and emitting deterministic JSON with a human projection over the same object.","decision":"1. **The Context Engine is a Runtime consumer.** It consumes Engineering Knowledge **only** through the runtime services — `Resolver.Resolve` (the focus), `Relations.Upstream` / `Relations.Downstream` / `Relations.From` / `Relations.To` (the neighborhood traversals), `Timeline.Line` (the history), `Knowledge.NumberForLine` (issue numbers). It **never touches the store, never parses Markdown, never reads the repository layout**, and it implements **no graph primitives of its own** beyond composing runtime calls — traversal is delegated to the Relations service (consumers never hand-walk relationships, ADR-014 §Decision 2). The import surface is `{runtime, exchange, conformance}` (conformance for the representation-independent ontology helpers), deliberately **not** `machine/`, `view/` or `store/` (Decision 11).\n\n2. **The Context Object (schema `eka-context-v1`) is a derived representation, NOT a CKO.** It is a projection around one subject: the **focus** carries full unit detail (the machine document shape minus `schema`/`author`/`created`/`updated`, plus the qualified line form); the neighborhood is carried as compact **Entry** references (canonical form, line form, type, id, domain, stratum, primary state, role, issue number) — **never full documents**. Consumers that need a full CKO fetch it via `eka get` (ADR-015). The context is a bounded lens over the project union, not a store and not a wrapper of the store.\n\n3. **Deterministic-first.** The engine is purely deterministic: **no LLM, no vector search, no randomness, no ambient state** — the canonical model only (identity, domain, stratum, state, relationships, metadata, history). Determinism contract (the machine interface's mirror): fixed struct field order (declaration order), stable schema string, sections and strata **sorted by canonical form**, collections **deduplicated by line form with first-role-wins**, **no timestamps, no host-dependent values**. The same subject, depth and options always produce the same Context Object. Future semantic retrieval (Decision 9) may **complement** the deterministic object, never replace it.\n\n4. **Depth scoping — bounded and predictable by design.** Three depths, ascending reach:\n   - **`local`** — the focus (full detail) plus its instance-line history; no relationships collected; one stratum (the focus's own).\n   - **`dependency`** — the **one-hop neighborhood**, classified into sections (Decision 5): upstream (units the focus's relationships point at), downstream (units that reference the focus), dependencies (depends-on / derives-from outgoing targets), constraints (higher-authority units), decisions, planning, review — plus the strata landscape of the collected set.\n   - **`engineering`** — everything of `dependency` PLUS a **bounded constraint closure**: higher-authority units (strata above the focus) reachable through the collected units' own outgoing relationships — **max 2 hops, at most 64 units** (`maxConstraintClosure`), higher-authority strata only, the focus line never a constraint, hop-2 units never expanded further. The cap is deterministic: the expansion stops as soon as 64 units are added.\n\n5. **Stratification preserved.** Every collected unit appears in exactly **one stratum** (its own domain's, grouped 1..5 ascending, only non-empty strata); **constraints** are the collected units with a stratum **strictly higher** than the focus's (a strictly smaller stratum number — the Stratum Authority Invariant of ADR-008). Section membership (edge role, type token) and stratum membership (authority) are two views of one collected set (Consequences: the redundancy is by design).\n\n6. **History comes from the Runtime Timeline — no separate history mechanism.** The history section is `Timeline.Line` mapped to compact entries (canonical form, instance version, revision, object hash, change log in occurrence order). History is derived, not maintained (ADR-011 §5.4); the Context Engine reads it through the Kernel like every other consumer.\n\n7. **The CLI `eka context` is a thin adapter.** Subject grammar is the Resolver grammar — the canonical form `\u003cns\u003e/\u003ctype\u003e:\u003cid\u003e:\u003cv\u003e` or the qualified line form `\u003cns\u003e/\u003ctype\u003e:\u003cid\u003e` (namespace required; unqualified forms refused) — plus the issue-number form `#\u003cn\u003e` (per-group incremental numbers, RFC-style; ambiguous numbers refused). Flags: `--depth` (default `dependency`), `--json`, `--compact` (implies `--json`), `--no-content`. Exit codes: `0` constructed; `1` workspace/repository-state refusal (no workspace, not an EKA repository — ADR-018, unregistered repository — the same refusal class as `eka get`, ADR-013/ADR-015); `2` usage (invalid subject or depth token) or internal error. Read-only end to end: `runtime.Open` (never `runtime.Ensure`), never creates a workspace.\n\n8. **One Context Object, two outputs — no per-consumer context generation.** The machine-readable JSON (`--json` / `--compact`) and the human projection (default output) are **both pure functions of the SAME Context Object**: the human renderer (`cmd/render_context.go`) is a thin renderer over the object — it never builds context itself, never touches the Runtime, and is a pure function of (object, style). A future consumer (MCP, an IDE, an agent runtime) consumes the same object; there is no separate context-generation path per consumer.\n\n9. **The AI boundary.** The Context Object is the deterministic contract at the AI boundary: **Context Object → future AI adapter → LLM**, model-agnostic. The engine never calls a model and never assumes one; any model or agent consumes the plain deterministic JSON, and future semantic capabilities (reranking, semantic neighbors, summarization) live on the far side of the boundary — as adapters over the object, never inside the engine.\n\n10. **Relationship to `eka get` and `eka view` — three consumers of the same Runtime.** `eka get` **retrieves** canonical knowledge (the machine interface, full CKOs); `eka view` **visualizes** it (the projection interface, presentational meaning); `eka context` **constructs understanding** (the context interface — a derived object around one subject). The context is **not a wrapper of `get`**: it builds a new object (focus detail + compact entries), while `get` returns full CKOs as they are; the context path shares only the Runtime API and the CKO model with the other two and never imports `machine/` or `view/` — the same formal separation ADR-015 §Decision 5 established.\n\n11. **Package layout.** `contexts/` — the engine (imports `runtime`, `exchange`, `conformance` only; deliberately not `machine/`/`view`/`store`); `cmd/context.go` — the CLI adapter; `cmd/render_context.go` — the human renderer. **No Runtime API extension was needed in v1**: composition of the existing services (Resolve, Upstream/Downstream/From/To, Line, NumberForLine) sufficed — the Kernel contract held for a third consumer class without boundary churn.\n\n12. **Known limitations (accepted).**\n    - **Draft notes are not in the store.** The engine resolves published units only: an unresolved depends-on / derives-from target is skipped (draft tolerance, the Resolver contract) — a draft `cmt-` note attached to the subject does not appear in the context until it is published.\n    - **The context is a store snapshot, not authoring state.** Like every projection it reflects the last sync (`eka sync` first) — live authoring edits are not visible (ADR-013).\n    - **N+1 resolves.** Per-collected-unit resolution and per-line issue-number lookups add read amplification at build time; acceptable at workspace scale — the ADR-014 scan-based trade-off, documented here for the same reason.\n    - **Ambiguous `#\u003cn\u003e` needs narrowing.** Per-group counters make an issue number ambiguous across groups; the CLI refuses ambiguous numbers deterministically (exit 2) with the identity grammar as the remedy.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  DB[(Runtime DB — eka.db\\nobject_payloads + object_refs)] --\u003e RUN[Runtime API — runtime/\\nResolver.Resolve · Relations.Upstream/Downstream/From/To\\nTimeline.Line · Knowledge.NumberForLine · ADR-014]\n  RUN --\u003e CTX[Context Engine — contexts/\\nBuild(subject, depth, options) · schema eka-context-v1\\nlocal | dependency | engineering]\n  CTX --\u003e OBJ[Context Object\\nfocus · strata · sections · history]\n  OBJ --\u003e J[eka context --json / --compact\\ncanonical JSON · stdout]\n  OBJ --\u003e R[Human renderer\\ncmd/render_context.go · terminal]\n  OBJ --\u003e AI[Future AI adapter → LLM\\nMCP · Atrium · agents\\nmodel-agnostic]\n```","consequences":"- **Positive**: bounded deterministic context — the engineering lens around one subject is predictable by construction (depth caps, sorted sections, deduplicated strata), never a full graph crawl.\n- **Positive**: model-agnostic foundation for MCP/Atrium/AI — the AI boundary is a plain deterministic JSON contract (schema `eka-context-v1`); semantic capabilities attach as adapters, not engine changes.\n- **Positive**: no boundary churn — v1 needed no Runtime API extension; composition of the existing services sufficed, proving the Kernel contract again (third consumer class).\n- **Positive**: reuse of the Runtime — stratification, traversal, timeline and integrity all come from the canonical services; nothing is re-implemented and no storage knowledge leaks into the engine.\n- **Negative / trade-off**: N+1 resolve cost — per-unit resolution plus per-line number lookups amplify reads per build; accepted at workspace scale (the ADR-014 scan-based trade-off, mirrored here).\n- **Negative / trade-off**: `Relations.To` / `Relations.Downstream` are scan-based in v0.2 (ADR-014) — every dependency-depth context build pays the workspace-wide reverse scan; deterministic and fine at runtime scale, indexed SQL reserved as the later optimization.\n- **Negative / trade-off**: redundancy between sections and strata — a unit appears in its section (edge role) and in its stratum (authority); by design, two views of one collected set, and the consumer picks the lens.\n- **Negative / trade-off**: ambiguous `#\u003cn\u003e` subjects need narrowing — per-group counters force the user to spell the identity when the number is not unique; deterministic refusal with the remedy in the message.\n\n### Future Extensibility (not implemented)\n\n- **Semantic retrieval complement** — reranking or semantic neighbors over the deterministic object; additive, on the far side of the AI boundary (Decision 9), never replacing the canonical construction.\n- **MCP / AI adapter** — a thin adapter mapping the Context Object into model prompts or an MCP tool surface; the object contract is the interface, unchanged.\n- **Deeper neighborhoods** — traversal beyond the bounded closure (2 hops / 64 units) as an additive depth token over the same Runtime API.\n- **Content shaping** — per-section content control or entry detail flags (additive over the same schema, the ADR-015 additive contract).\n\n### References\n\n- [ADR-014](adr-014-runtime-interface-architecture.md) — the ADR this one derives from: the Runtime Kernel; the Runtime API services the Context Engine consumes; §Future Extensibility reserved the Context Engine slot this ADR realizes\n- [ADR-015](adr-015-machine-retrieval-interface.md) — the ADR this one derives from: the machine interface whose deterministic JSON conventions (schema string, fixed field order, sorted collections, no timestamps) and `eka get` full-CKO retrieval the Context Object mirrors; §Future Extensibility reserved context generation\n- [ADR-012](adr-012-canonical-knowledge-object-runtime.md) — the CKO (`exchange.Unit`) the engine reads through the Runtime; the CKO-only runtime the context path inherits\n- [ADR-011](adr-011-immutable-engineering-knowledge-model.md) — the immutable store behind the Kernel; `object_hash` in the focus and history entries; history is derived (`Timeline.Line`), never maintained\n- [ADR-008](adr-008-engineering-domain-model.md) — Engineering Domain and Knowledge Stratum derivation (`DomainForToken`, `Stratum`) — the strata landscape and the constraints semantics (strictly higher authority)\n- [ADR-013](adr-013-store-backed-projections.md) — the sync-first precondition and the exit-`1` refusal class `eka context` mirrors\n- [ADR-016](adr-016-standard-v2-json-authoring.md) — spec-standard-v2 §5: the camelCase convention (`eka-context-v1` keys) and the canonical content object (`fields` vs legacy `text`)\n- [ADR-018](adr-018-repository-context.md) — the not-an-EKA-repository gate (`no eka.yaml`) `eka context` enforces\n- The Context Engine — `contexts/` (implemented in parallel with this ADR; this document is authoritative for its architecture): [`../../contexts/engine.go`](../../contexts/engine.go) (Build, depths, collection, constraint closure), [`../../contexts/object.go`](../../contexts/object.go) (the Context Object, schema `eka-context-v1`, serializers), [`../../contexts/helpers.go`](../../contexts/helpers.go) (entry mapping, primary state, strata grouping), [`../../contexts/context_test.go`](../../contexts/context_test.go)\n- The CLI adapter — [`../../cmd/context.go`](../../cmd/context.go); the human renderer — [`../../cmd/render_context.go`](../../cmd/render_context.go)\n- Runtime services consumed — `Resolve`: [`../../runtime/resolver.go`](../../runtime/resolver.go); `From`/`To`/`Upstream`/`Downstream`: [`../../runtime/relations.go`](../../runtime/relations.go); `Line`: [`../../runtime/timeline.go`](../../runtime/timeline.go); `NumberForLine`: [`../../runtime/knowledge.go`](../../runtime/knowledge.go)\n- Runtime interface document (context section updated in parallel): [`../runtime-architecture.md`](../runtime-architecture.md)\n- CLI contract and exit codes: [`../cli.md`](../cli.md)\n- ADR index: [`../adr-summary.md`](../adr-summary.md)","alternativesConsidered":"- **LLM/semantic context generation now** — rejected: nondeterministic and unbounded; deterministic-first is the foundation the semantic layer builds on (Decisions 3 and 9), and no model should be required to read a context.\n- **Vector search for neighborhood discovery** — rejected: the canonical relationship graph is exact and complete; embeddings would substitute approximate, mutable results where exact traversal already exists (`Relations.Upstream`/`Downstream`).\n- **Full-document sections (embedded CKOs)** — rejected: payload bloat and unbounded objects; compact Entry references plus `eka get` keep the object bounded (Decision 2).\n- **A graph engine inside `contexts/`** — rejected: hand-walking relationships would duplicate the Relations service and break the consumer contract (ADR-014 §Decision 2).\n- **Extend `eka get` with context flags** — rejected: mixes retrieval and construction in one contract; the context is a distinct consumer with its own object, schema and human projection (Decision 10).\n- **One depth containing everything** — rejected: unbounded; depth scoping (Decision 4) is what keeps the context predictable."}